COMPLETENESS. The rules specify:
- the deck (30 cards);
- the deal by recorded order, and the hand-2 re-deal from the same order;
- slot order and how the hand closes up after a draw;
- legality (strictly higher number; Wager only before any number);
- the illegal-play penalty;
- the draw-source fallback (empty pile, or the card just discarded);
- the announcement content;
- the scoring order (S, Wager x1.5 rounded down, +10 at 5+ cards, embargo halving positives) and end-of-hand reveals.

DRAW PILE NEVER RUNS OUT. At most one card leaves the 20-card pile per turn. Before the draw on turn 20, at least 20-19 = 1 card remains, so the stated invariant holds and the game is finite: 40 rounds.

SMALL AMBIGUITY (not fatal). Does an illegally played card, which lands on its discard pile, count as 'the card the player discarded this turn' for the no-retake rule? Reading 'discard announcement' together with 'goes onto its discard pile', a referee would rule yes. Stating it explicitly would remove all doubt.

DECIDED BY ARITHMETIC. Scoring and the winner are pure arithmetic on recorded columns, penalties and embargoes. No judge, board or clock is needed. Embargo is a simultaneous secret poll, which is correct.

LIMITS (all within):
- steps 13 of max 20
- choice steps 3 of max 4
- attributes 19 of max 20
- hidden elements 3 of max 3 (hands, embargoes, deck order)
- one shuffle in round 1, within 1 random draw per round
- rounds 40 of max 40

OTHER CHECKS. No unsupported feature: no trading, no binding agreements, no conditional blocks, no board. It is a two-player game, so no talk phase is needed. It is not a repeated cooperation game, so the late-round betrayal test does not apply.
